# join → SQL JOIN Translation

## KQL Join Syntax

```kql
T1 | join kind=inner (T2) on KeyColumn
T1 | join kind=leftouter (T2) on Key1, Key2
```

## Join Kind Mapping

| KQL kind | SQL |
|---|---|
| `inner` (default) | `INNER JOIN` |
| `leftouter` | `LEFT OUTER JOIN` |
| `rightouter` | `RIGHT OUTER JOIN` |
| `fullouter` | `FULL OUTER JOIN` |

## ON Clause

KQL's `on` keyword specifies shared key columns.
SQL requires explicit `table.column = table.column` references.

```kql
AppLogs | join (Users) on UserId
→ SELECT * FROM AppLogs INNER JOIN Users ON AppLogs.UserId = Users.UserId
```

## Multiple Keys

```kql
Orders | join (Customers) on CustomerId, Region
→ INNER JOIN Customers ON Orders.CustomerId = Customers.CustomerId
  AND Orders.Region = Customers.Region
```

## KQL Join Flavors NOT in v0.1

KQL has many join flavors (innerunique, leftanti, rightanti, leftsemi, etc.).
v0.1 supports only: inner, leftouter, rightouter, fullouter.

If any other flavor detected → keep in KQL engine.

## Jagged Risk Note

The `on` clause maps column names assuming the column exists with the same
name in both tables. If a query uses column aliasing before the join,
the generated ON clause may be wrong. Human review required when
extend/project appears before a join.

## Known Limits

- join with complex subqueries in the right-hand side → partial support
- innerunique, leftanti, rightanti, leftsemi → NOT SUPPORTED v0.1
- Cross-join (no `on`) → NOT SUPPORTED
